    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4638",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Subsistence practices of people can be determined by recovering and studying:\n\nOptions: \nA. geological layers and sediment types.\nB. the architectural style and layout of structures on the site.\nC. pumice and evidence of pyroclastic surges.\nD. the faunal assemblage from a site.\nE. an osteological comparative collection from a site.\nF. evidence of past volcanic activity.\nG. all of the above.\nH. historical records and documents related to the site.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine subsistence practices—how people obtained and consumed food—we need evidence of what they ate and how they procured it. The faunal assemblage (animal remains) from a site directly informs us about the types of animals consumed, hunting practices, and dietary patterns. While other options like geological layers, architecture, or volcanic evidence may provide environmental or cultural context, they do not directly reveal subsistence behaviors.\n\nThus, the most direct and relevant evidence comes from the faunal assemblage.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
